<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Gaeta</h1><p class="st-meta">839 CE – 1140 CE (approx.) · capital Gaeta</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Small coastal state between Rome and Naples that broke free of Byzantium in the 9th century and lasted until the Normans absorbed it in 1140.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaeta">Gaeta</a>, a fortified promontory on the Tyrrhenian coast, was part of Byzantine territory loosely tied to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-naples">Duchy of Naples</a>. In the 9th century, amid Arab raids and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> pressure, it gained autonomy under local rulers styled hypatoi (consuls). Around 867 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/docibilis-i-of-gaeta">Docibilis I</a> founded the dynasty that made it a hereditary state.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaeta">Gaeta</a>, who later took the title of duke, controlled a small territory around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-gaeta">Gulf of Gaeta</a> and grew rich from sea trade. They sometimes allied with Arab raiders settled at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garigliano">Garigliano</a>, then joined the Christian coalition that destroyed that base in 915. The duchy minted its own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> coins, and its charters, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/codex-diplomaticus-cajetanus">Codex Caietanus</a>, document its society.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From 1032 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaeta">Gaeta</a> fell under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> princes of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-capua">Capua</a> and then under Norman lords. In 1140 it submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-ii-of-sicily">Roger II</a> and joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-sicily">Kingdom of Sicily</a>, keeping a degree of municipal autonomy.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-gaeta.jpg" alt="Duchy of Gaeta" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Facquis · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Gaeta_(10th_century).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>839 CE – 1140 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Gaeta</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Docibilis I, John I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ducatus Caietanus</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=990&amp;focus=duchy-of-gaeta" aria-label="Duchy of Gaeta on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5011 707 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Gaeta lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5331 963L5349 976M5331 963L5349 965M5331 963L5333 1064"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5349 976h0M5349 965h0M5333 1064h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5331 963h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5331 963h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Gaeta. <a href="/atlas?year=990&amp;focus=duchy-of-gaeta">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
